html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Cell compartments and a real fibroblast</title><style>body{margin:0;background:#121a1e;color:#e5e8e4;font:18px/1.55 Georgia,serif}main{max-width:1200px;margin:auto;padding:clamp(1rem,4vw,3rem)}h1{line-height:1.1;font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3.6rem);margin:.3em 0}h2{font-size:1.35rem;margin:.4em 0}a{color:#9ad8d0}.eyebrow{color:#a6d3c8;letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;font:700 .8rem sans-serif}.intro{max-width:75ch;color:#bdcbc9}.grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1.5rem;margin-top:2rem}figure{margin:0;background:#202d30;border:1px solid #4a605e;padding:1rem}figure img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}figcaption{font-size:.93rem;margin-top:1rem}small{color:#c3d2ce} .photo{background:#050505;position:relative}.photo img{width:100%}.key{display:flex;gap:1.2rem;flex-wrap:wrap;font: .9rem sans-serif;padding:.7rem;color:#fff}.yellow{color:#ffdb50}.red{color:#ff7777}.tissue{margin-top:1.5rem}.tissue img{max-width:760px}.notes{max-width:90ch;border-top:1px solid #506461;margin-top:2rem;padding-top:1rem}@media(max-width:750px){.grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}@media print{body{background:white;color:#111}figure{background:white;border-color:#777;break-inside:avoid}a{color:#124c55}.intro,small{color:#333}.grid{gap:.5rem}}</style></head><body><main><div class="eyebrow">Chapter 01 / cells and tissues</div><h1>From cell compartments to tissue</h1><p class="intro">A conceptual map helps us discuss compartments. A microscope image records selected signals from a particular specimen. The images show different specimens; the drawing is a conceptual model.</p><div class="grid"><figure><h2>A. Conceptual cutaway</h2><img src="ch01-cell-schematic.svg" alt="Schematic animal cell showing the plasma membrane, nucleus and nuclear pores, endoplasmic reticulum continuous with the nuclear envelope, Golgi, mitochondrial network, lysosome, peroxisome and cytoskeletal filaments."><figcaption>Original diagram. Shapes, sizes, numbers, positions and colours are illustrative; cells vary. The outer nuclear membrane is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum. The pores regulate transport between nucleus and cytoplasm. No scale is implied.</figcaption></figure><figure><h2>B. Actual confocal micrograph</h2><div class="photo"><img src="ch01-cell-reference.jpg" alt="Confocal micrograph of a primary human fibroblast: yellow staining marks the nucleus and red staining shows mitochondria as a branching network."><div class="key"><span class="yellow">● Nucleus · yellow stain</span><span class="red">● Mitochondria · red stain</span></div></div><figcaption><em>Primary human fibroblast.</em> Matthew Daniels / University of Oxford; <a href="https://wellcomecollection.org/works/fmsmxyyh">Wellcome Collection, B0007852</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. JPEG unmodified; labels added separately and image displayed at reduced resolution. Physical calibration and live/fixed status are not supplied by the record.</figcaption></figure></div><figure class="tissue"><h2>C. Actual tissue section: human skin</h2><img src="ch01-cell-tissue-reference.jpg" alt="Stained human skin section, showing layered tissue and fibrous material with purple, green and yellow staining."><figcaption><em>Human skin section, histology.</em> Stained with aldehyde fuchsin, Orange G and Light Green; the archive identifies elastic fibres as purple and collagen as green to yellow. Full image field width: 400 μm, according to the archive record. Kevin Mackenzie, University of Aberdeen; <a href="https://wellcomecollection.org/works/pbfv4uhw">Wellcome Collection, B0009427</a>, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. JPEG unmodified; entire field retained and displayed at reduced resolution. Fixation, section thickness, body site, donor and instrument are not supplied. The archive does not explicitly specify optical modality.</figcaption></figure><p class="notes"><strong>Reading the evidence.</strong> The fluorescent colours identify selected stains, not ordinary visible colours. The fibroblast image does not show every organelle or establish the complete cell boundary; it is neither an intact tissue section nor a three-dimensional reconstruction. No depth or physical dimensions can be inferred from it. The skin section is a separate specimen; its stained colours are not natural living-tissue colours. The drawing is not traced or reconstructed from either image.</p><small>Structure references: <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9927/">nuclear envelope and pores</a>; <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9889/">endoplasmic reticulum</a>; <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26907/">cell compartmentalization</a>.</small></main></body></html>'''
